 ## Background
-The concept of removing interior polygons dates back to early voxel-based terrain generation, such as in Minecraft-like engines. In these systems, a brute-force approach of instantiating individual cube models was replaced by reconstructing cubes from triangles and removing sides adjacent to solid neighbors [S1, unofficial]. This optimization was critical because rendering all 12 triangles (6 sides) for every cube was inefficient when only 2 triangles (1 side) were visible [S1, unofficial]. The meshes of individual cubes were combined into larger "chunks" to further optimize implementation, though this introduced constraints such as vertex limits in early graphics APIs like Unity's [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of removing interior polygons was introduced in early voxel engines to improve rendering performance. The original approach involved instantiating models of cubes, which was inefficient. A class that creates cubes from triangles was added to allow each cube to check whether it has a solid neighbor and determine which sides to render [S1, unofficial]. If a neighbor existed, the side adjacent to that neighbor was not rendered [S1, unofficial]. To further optimize, the meshes of all cubes were combined into a single big mesh called a 'chunk' [S1, unofficial]. This introduced constraints, such as Unity's limit of ~65,000 vertices per mesh, requiring chunks to be stacked or split [S1, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in voxel-based games like Minecraft and its clones to handle large worlds efficiently.
 
